[size=6][b]After years of being destroyed by your infection mods, the villagers decided it was time to build their own.[/b][/size]

[b]The Village Fights Back[/b] turns ordinary villages into self-organizing strongholds. At the heart of every village sits the [b]Village Brain Node[/b], an AI that spends the village's emeralds to arm its people, raise walls, and evolve its defenders across [b]4 stages[/b], until the village can strike back at whatever threatens it, players included.

Built to stand against the harshest infection mods, the village answers with its own: a contagious counter-infection, [b]12 weapons[/b], [b]17 new creatures[/b] (two of them bosses), [b]3 potions[/b], [b]17 structures[/b] it raises by itself, and a whole [b]new dimension[/b] to chase the fight into.

[size=5][b]The Village Brain Node[/b][/size]

Every village gets a [b]Village Brain Node[/b], a block that appears [b]in place of the village bell[/b] at the heart of the settlement and runs the whole defense. It keeps a [b]per-village economy[/b]: the emeralds the village earns, and the ones you donate, fund everything it does.

[b]It evolves through 4 stages.[/b] Each stage it reaches arms and upgrades the village's villagers, guards, and iron golems with stronger gear and new abilities. Let it grow on its own over time, or [b]feed it emeralds to push it through the stages faster[/b].

[b]It thinks for itself.[/b] The Brain reads the state of the village and reacts: it sends out settlers, raises walls, and builds specific structures such as golem factories, barracks, and lure towers when the situation calls for them, spending emeralds as it goes. Right-click it to open its screen anytime, showing the current stage, stored emeralds, and live villager, guard, and golem counts. A [b]Logs[/b] tab lists the last actions the Brain took, with the day each one happened.

[b]Where you stand with the village.[/b] The Brain is [b]neutral to you[/b] by default. Attack a villager, strike the node, or [b]steal from the village's chests[/b], and you become an [b]Enemy of the Village[/b]: its defenders turn on you and hunt you down. Earn its trust instead by [b]donating emeralds[/b] to the Brain or helping the village [b]survive a raid[/b], and you become a [b]Hero of the Village[/b], which grants you [b]temporary permission to open and loot its chests[/b]. Chests and barrels placed near the Brain are claimed by the village, so taking from them without Hero status counts as theft, and the village will know.

[b]It protects its own.[/b] At sunset the village calls in reinforcements scaled to the Brain's current stage, and an [b]immunity aura[/b] radiates from the node over the village's villagers, guards, and golems.

[size=5][b]Armed defenders and the arsenal[/b][/size]

The village does not run. [b]Every villager fights[/b], the [b]guards[/b] carry heavier weapons, and the [b]iron golems[/b] wield rocket launchers. They hold their ground against any hostile mob, and because targeting keys off Minecraft's own "hostile" tag, [b]monsters from other mods are handled automatically[/b], with no special support needed.

They fight as a unit. Defenders are [b]immune to friendly fire[/b] and never hurt one another, and the golems only loose a rocket when no ally stands near the target, so the blast never catches their own. Golems brawl up close, [b]launch themselves skyward on a wind charge[/b], and fire from the top of the arc. Strike a defender yourself and it [b]turns on you[/b] briefly before calming down, and you can read the mood on its face: a defender in combat wears an angry expression.

As the Brain evolves, so does their gear. Higher stages hand defenders steadily stronger equipment and abilities. From [b]stage 3[/b] their blades begin to [b]infect whatever they hit[/b] with the Emerald Virus, escalating to a stronger strain at [b]stage 4[/b], while ordinary iron golems become golden [b]Gigachad Golems[/b] with far more health and rockets of their own.

[b]The arsenal the village forges includes:[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Alien Slasher[/b] and [b]Alien Eradicator[/b], blades that spread the Emerald Virus (I and II) on hit
[*]a native [b]Mace[/b] with its three enchantments (Density, Breach, Wind Burst), and its Limbo-forged upgrade the [b]Alien Smasher[/b]
[*]the [b]Rocket Launcher[/b], [b]Wind Charge[/b], and [b]Fire Grenade[/b]
[*][b]Emerald Virus Arrows[/b] (I and II)
[*]full [b]Emerald[/b] and [b]Limbo[/b] gear lines, tools and armor alike, including the [b]Emerald Hammer[/b] and a [b]Limbo chestplate that doubles as an elytra[/b]
[/list]

Most of it is craftable, the [b]Weaponsmith[/b] sells the Mace, wind charges, and the mace-enchant books, and defenders have a chance to drop their weapon when they fall.

[size=5][b]Walls and defensive structures[/b][/size]

As the village grows, the Brain fortifies it. A built-in planner traces the village's actual footprint, however irregular, and [b]villagers raise a wall around it course by course[/b], complete with gates, [b]loopholes to shoot through[/b], and scaffolding to reach the high spots. The wall scales to the size of the settlement.

From [b]stage 2[/b], [b]Wall Defenders[/b] are mounted above the gates: turret creatures that fire real bullets and rockets at anything that comes near.

The Brain builds with materials of its own:
[list]
[*][b]Reinforced glass[/b], as tough as ancient debris yet still lets light through, used for protective domes
[*][b]Protecting glass[/b], which stops projectiles coming from outside while the village keeps firing out
[*][b]Fake cobblestone[/b] and [b]fake obsidian[/b] smart gates, which allies pass straight through but enemies cannot
[/list]

And it raises purpose-built structures as the situation demands: [b]golem factories[/b] that forge new golems, [b]barracks[/b], and [b]lure towers[/b] that funnel hostile mobs onto powder snow and drop them into a pool of lava. To put all this up quickly, the village uses special charges: [b]Blue TNT[/b] that builds a house block by block, and [b]Green TNT[/b] that builds tower variants.

[size=5][b]The Emerald Virus[/b][/size]

The village answers infection with infection of its own. The [b]Emerald Virus[/b] is a contagious green growth that slowly converts the world around it into [b]Fertile blocks[/b] (with richer [b]Rich[/b] variants), a full family of green-veined dirt, stone, sand, wood, deepslate, netherrack, end stone, sculk, leaves, and more. The spread is deliberately slow and capped. Those blocks are not just decoration: [b]some of them hold emeralds[/b], feeding the very economy the Brain runs on. The growth even has its own [b]fertile chorus plants[/b], whose fruit you can eat (it teleports you like ordinary chorus fruit, with a rare and unfortunate side effect).

The virus is also a weapon. Once a creature is infected it [b]stays infected and spreads the strain on contact[/b], until it is [b]cured by the mod's Immunity[/b] (as an effect or a potion). You can deliver it with the [b]Emerald Apple[/b] (eat it at your peril, or brew it into a [b]Potion of Emerald Virus[/b] with splash, lingering, and tipped-arrow forms), with the [b]Emerald Virus Arrows[/b], or with the infecting blades the defenders carry.

At its peak the virus produces the [b]Emeraldger[/b], a stage-4 virus warrior whose strength [b]scales with its host's health[/b] and who is [b]immune to fire and to every status effect[/b] (the virus included), staring out through glowing green eyes. And out among the fertile fields you may meet the [b]Rich Sheep[/b], a green-touched sheep that drops lime wool and an emerald.

[size=5][b]Bosses and the Infected faction[/b][/size]

Two boss-grade creatures can take the field, both born of the village itself: its champion in life, and its vengeful spirit in death.

[b]The Vindictive Village Spirit.[/b] When a village's Brain Node falls, whether its last villager dies or the node itself is destroyed, the village does not pass quietly. It rises as the [b]Vindictive Village Spirit[/b], a boss bent on vengeance that hammers the area with green sonic-boom [b]banish waves[/b], a ground-shaking [b]slam[/b], and a screen-shaking roar. It leads an [b]Infected faction[/b], the village's fallen defenders turned vengeful: [b]vindictive guards[/b] and [b]vindictive gigachads[/b] that [b]never fight one another[/b] and shrug off most explosion damage, and any creature struck down by a [b]direct hit from the spirit rises again as one of its minions[/b]. Let a village fall and you inherit a very angry ghost.

[b]General Darius.[/b] The village's living champion. Only a [b]stage-4[/b] Brain spends the emeralds to march him out, and only against a true threat: an enemy with [b]100 or more health[/b], or a player who has killed [b]three or more[/b] villagers or guards within a few minutes. Darius wields the [b]Alien Smasher[/b] and the full [b]Limbo set[/b] (elytra chestplate included), so he gets every Limbo advantage: immune to fire and falling, clears harmful effects, breaks through soft blocks on contact, and below half health he opens up with a [b]banish beam[/b]. He [b]locks onto the single biggest threat[/b] and ignores everyone else, hunting it across the world before turning to the next, then walks home once the danger is gone. Along the way he [b]conscripts idle guards[/b] into a squad, double-jumps straight through ceilings, and pillars out of any pit. He carries his own boss bar, and he [b]cannot be banished[/b].

[size=5][b]The Limbo dimension[/b][/size]

Beyond the war for the village lies [b]the Limbo[/b], a dark void dimension with a bedrock holding cell at its spawn. There are several ways in and out: drink a [b]Limbo Potion[/b] to be banished, throw a [b]Volatile Limbo Potion[/b] to banish others, or build a [b]Green Limbo Portal[/b] by lighting a fake-obsidian frame. The portal runs at [b]twice the Nether's compression[/b], so a short walk in the Limbo covers a long way back home. When you are sent there, a [b]Limbo Gravel[/b] marker is left where you stood, and [b]destroying that marker[/b] pulls you back; you can also [b]use a bed[/b] in the Limbo, the dedicated Limbo Bed or any bed at all, to return. Even falling out of the world leads here: the void drops you into the Limbo, then from the Limbo into the End, and only the End's void is fatal.

[b]What waits in the dark.[/b] The Limbo is dotted with [b]floating bedrock cubes[/b] of every size, their outer shells crusted with [b]Forgotten Glowstone[/b], a vivid green growth that lights the void. The larger cubes hide [b]nests of ancient debris[/b] cradling [b]Limbo Eggs[/b], and those eggs hatch into [b]Limbo Phantoms[/b]: tripled in size, glowing, and able to [b]phase straight through bedrock[/b] to snatch creatures and haul them off. Hunt them for [b]Limbo Membrane[/b]. The Limbo is not even fully sealed from your world: a small fraction of its chunks [b]mirror their Overworld and Nether twins[/b], copying that terrain in, after which blocks you change on either side are mirrored across, though living creatures never make the crossing.

[b]Limbo gear.[/b] Craft Limbo Membrane into [b]Limbo Ingots[/b], then use a rare [b]smithing template[/b] to forge netherite into the [b]Limbo set[/b]:
[list]
[*][b]Limbo armor[/b] with full knockback immunity, including a [b]chestplate that doubles as an elytra[/b]
[*]a [b]Limbo tool set[/b] that out-mines netherite, with a [b]pickaxe that breaks bedrock[/b] and reinforced deepslate
[*]wearing the [b]full set[/b] makes you immune to fire and falling, clears harmful effects, turns the Limbo Phantoms neutral, smashes soft blocks as you move, lets you [b]walk through bedrock[/b], and arms a keybound green [b]banish beam[/b] on a 30 second cooldown
[/list]

[b]The Limbo Sage.[/b] A new villager profession that deals in rare and costly Limbo goods, from oddment blocks up to enchanted Limbo tools, though [b]never Limbo armor[/b]. Threaten one and it hurls a [b]Volatile Limbo Potion[/b] to banish you outright.

[b]The Limbo raid.[/b] Linger too long in the Limbo and you carry a curse home: come back to a village and a [b]Limbo raid[/b] descends, a ghostly, oversized version of the vanilla raid roster, far tougher than the mobs it imitates. The whole journey is charted by its own advancements.

[size=5][b]Where you stand: Hero or Enemy of the Village[/b][/size]

The village keeps score of how you treat it, and it responds in kind.

[b]Become a Hero.[/b] Donate emeralds to the Brain Node and you earn [b]Hero of the Village[/b], one minute per emerald, stacking onto whatever time you have left, in any game mode. Helping the village [b]win a raid[/b] earns it the usual way too. As a Hero you are trusted, and may freely open and take from the village's chests.

[b]Become an Enemy.[/b] Cross the village and you are branded an [b]Enemy of the Village for 20 minutes[/b]: strike a villager, guard, or golem, or steal from its stores. Any chest or barrel placed within a Brain Node's range is [b]claimed by the village[/b]. Open one without Hero standing and you get a warning; take something anyway and the whole server hears that you are [b]stealing from the village[/b], your Hero standing is revoked, and every defender turns to hunt you. The mark sticks through milk and even the mod's own Immunity, and clears only when the timer runs out or you die.

[size=5][b]Requirements and credits[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Minecraft:[/b] 1.20.1
[*][b]Mod loader:[/b] Forge
[*][b]Required dependency:[/b] [b]Guard Villagers[/b] (the village's guards are built on it). Playtested with guardvillagers-1.20.1-1.6.17.
[*][b]Optional dependency:[/b] [b]Minecraft Comes Alive (MCA Reborn)[/b]. Install it and the whole village becomes MCA humans -- settlers, brain-node reinforcements and spawn-egg defenders all spawn as MCA villagers, and they take the mod's roles, weapons, walls and virus exactly as vanilla villagers do. Nothing changes if you do not install it. Tested against minecraft-comes-alive-7.7.0-beta.2+1.20.1.
